1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:09,103
The brush reveals a set of cubic stone weights. In the early layers they are neat and standard. In the later layers they are gone.

2
00:00:09,103 --> 00:00:14,977
The cities that once used them were fading. Why did the great cities fade away?

3
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:34,875
How do we know? We read the soil.

4
00:00:34,875 --> 00:00:40,723
Archaeologists dug up late layers at Harappa and other sites, and study old river beds and climate.

5
00:00:40,723 --> 00:00:45,145
The script is still unread, so no document tells us why.

6
00:01:00,417 --> 00:01:09,256
Around nineteen hundred BCE, the mature cities declined. This is called the Late Harappan phase. Town planning weakened.

7
00:01:09,256 --> 00:01:18,596
The drains were not kept up. Standard weights and the script go out of use. Long trade routes shrink. People did not vanish.

8
00:01:18,596 --> 00:01:27,070
Many moved east, toward the Ganga and Yamuna plains, and south into Gujarat and Maharashtra. Villages replaced cities.

9
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:35,234
At Harappa, a later cemetery, called Cemetery H, shows new burial customs. Rivers matter in this story.

10
00:01:35,234 --> 00:01:42,019
The Ghaggar-Hakra river system seems to have dried up in parts. The Indus is also known to shift its course.

11
00:01:42,019 --> 00:01:48,645
In 1944, the archaeologist Mortimer Wheeler suggested that invaders destroyed the cities.

12
00:01:48,645 --> 00:01:57,724
Most scholars today find that evidence weak. The question is still open, and historians keep testing each idea against new finds.

13
00:02:12,983 --> 00:02:22,155
Scholars disagree. Why did the cities decline? One view stresses the environment: changing climate, drying rivers and floods.

14
00:02:22,155 --> 00:02:31,262
Another view stresses society: the end of long-distance trade and the breakdown of city life. Many scholars combine the two.

15
00:02:46,517 --> 00:02:56,375
Carve this tip into memory. Rivers, trade, no invaders:. cities became villages. Remember rivers, trade and movement.

16
00:02:56,375 --> 00:02:59,717
Do not blame a single cause in your answer.

17
00:03:14,983 --> 00:03:31,374
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name these standard cubes that disappear in the Late Harappan phase. The answer: weights.

18
00:03:31,374 --> 00:03:45,048
Number two. Which later cemetery at Harappa shows new burial customs? The answer: Cemetery H. Number three.

19
00:03:45,048 --> 00:03:59,543
Which archaeologist suggested in 1944 that invaders destroyed the cities? The answer: Mortimer Wheeler. Now one line for Mains.

20
00:03:59,543 --> 00:04:04,464
Critically examine the causes of the decline of the Harappan civilisation.

21
00:04:23,717 --> 00:04:30,651
The chert weights go into their case. They once helped to keep trade fair. Three facts to take away.

22
00:04:30,651 --> 00:04:39,073
Cubes of chert, in neat ratios. Standard weights fade after 1900 BCE. No single cause is accepted.

23
00:04:39,073 --> 00:04:42,612
Next gallery: The Early Vedic Age.

